## Data Provenance, Inventory, and Extraction Constraints

Provenance. Extraction is based on Chefaa’s medications category and individual product pages across Egypt (eg-ar) and Saudi Arabia (sa-ar) locales. Pages present structured fields such as names (Arabic with occasional English), brand/manufacturer, strength, pack size, price, and availability; richer clinical fields (indications, dosing, contraindications, side effects, interactions, storage) appear consistently in some classes and variably in others. This variability informs both field mapping and a staged validation plan.

Inventory posture. The 130-page corpus has gaps and zero-result ranges; therefore, enumeration is based on ascending pagination with gap awareness, deduplication, and canonicalization before slicing to products ≥1401. Subcategory summaries (nine groups; 168 products) are used to cross-check taxonomy and provide fallback clinical content when detail pages lack key fields.

Constraints. Constructed URLs require validation against live pages; some product pages exhibit partial content for clinical fields and safety data. Strong localization (ar and en content across markets) demands normalization of language, currency, and dosage units. These constraints are explicitly mitigated by validation rules, alias handling, and targeted re-crawls for high-importance classes (e.g., antibiotics, anticoagulants, antihypertensives, diabetes).

![Listing architecture used for link harvesting](/workspace/docs/chefaa_homepage/chefaa_medications_listing.png)

The listing architecture above confirms consistent product card structures and slugs, which supports URL reconstruction and alternative link discovery when dynamic content or partial listings occur.[^2]

### Provenance Mapping and Link Harvesting

When product detail links are missing from listing records, candidate URLs are constructed from the canonical product path and slugs inferred from titles or names. Given variability in slug formatting and the presence of multiple locales, candidate URLs require validation. Practical mitigations include:

- Harvesting multiple slug variants and comparing resolved page titles.
- Preferring eg-ar or sa-ar product pages with consistent field presence.
- Using subcategory breadcrumbs to corroborate class and form when details are sparse.[^2]

These tactics reduce failed fetches and support sustained throughput, especially under constrained steps.

