### Normalization and Category Controls

- Names. Strip Arabic diacritics, collapse whitespace, and generate Latin transliterations with a preferred English title case.
- Dosage and packaging. Normalize to “X mg,” “X mg/5 ml,” or “X%”; unify pack_size to “N units” (e.g., “21 tablets”).
- Category mapping. Map to controlled therapeutic vocabulary (e.g., “Antibiotic (Penicillin combination)”, “NSAID”, “GLP-1 agonist”, “Osmotic laxative”). Use subcategory to resolve ambiguous classes.
- Brand normalization. Group script/spacing variants; for generics, prefer richer clinical record while preserving aliases for auditability.

## Extracted Evidence: Product Summaries and Calibration

The following summaries anchor the schema against real pages, calibrate clinical field coverage, and illustrate pricing anchors across markets.

Antibiotics: Augmentin 625 mg (10 tablets). The product page provides basic identification (brand: GSK; strength; pack size) and price (117 EGP). Clinical details are limited on this page; the leaflet is referenced for full prescribing information. This is sufficient for identification and pricing, while indications and safety require confirmation via label/leaflet.[^4]

Antibiotics: Floxamo 1 g (16 tablets). The page provides comprehensive clinical content: fixed-dose amoxicillin 500 mg + flucloxacillin 500 mg; broad-spectrum indications (respiratory, bone/joint, ENT, gastrointestinal, gallbladder, typhoid, kidney, prostatitis, gonorrhea, skin/soft tissue, post-surgical wounds, intra-abdominal, dental abscess, middle ear, bronchitis, pneumonia, paratyphoid); adult dosing (1 g every 8 hours, oral); administration advice (swallow whole; preferably 1 hour before or 2 hours after food); contraindications (penicillin allergy); side effects (mild, transient diarrhea, indigestion); pregnancy/lactation (consult a physician); interactions (anticoagulants, other antibiotics); renal impairment precautions; and storage guidance (general). Pricing: 110 EGP per pack; 55 EGP per strip.[^5]

NSAIDs: Sapofen 400 mg (20 tablets). The page provides robust detail: ibuprofen 400 mg; cyclooxygenase inhibition (COX-1 & COX-2) mechanism; indications (fever, headaches/migraines, muscle pain, arthritis, cold/flu aches, menstrual pain, dental pain, bone pain, lower back pain); dosing (1–2 tablets up to three times daily; max 6 tablets/day; not >10 days); administration (after food; avoid stomach irritation); contraindications (hypersensitivity, post-heart surgeries, peptic ulcers, severe hypertension, lupus, pregnancy); precautions (elderly, breastfeeding); side effects (common: GI upset, heartburn, nausea/vomiting, gas, diarrhea/constipation, headache, dizziness; rare/serious: ulcers, allergic reactions, cardiovascular events, bleeding/bruising, hypertension, anemia, severe skin reactions, vision changes, liver/kidney damage); safety note (no drowsiness). Manufacturer: SPIMACO. Price: 10 SAR. Alternatives listed.[^6]

NSAIDs: Nurofen children’s syrup (strawberry, 150 ml). The page specifies ibuprofen (NSAID), with analgesic/antipyretic/anti-inflammatory properties; indications (pain, inflammation, fever in adults and children, colds/flu, toothache, headache, menstrual pain, joint/muscle inflammation); dosing (5–15 ml up to three times daily; dose tailored to weight/age; temporary use only; not for long-term); formulation features (pleasant strawberry flavor; also available orange). Pricing: 17.15 SAR (with alternative 15.3 SAR noted). Market: Saudi Arabia.[^8]

Diabetes: Victoza (liraglutide 6 mg/ml; 2 pens × 3 ml). The page provides comprehensive content: GLP-1 agonist mechanism; indications (Type 2 diabetes in adults and children ≥10 years; adjunct to diet/exercise; cardiovascular risk reduction in adults with established CVD; weight loss via appetite regulation and delayed gastric emptying); dosage (once daily; subcutaneous injection; abdomen/thigh/upper arm; rotate sites; do not mix with insulin; do not share pens); side effects (common: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, appetite loss, constipation; serious: pancreatitis, hypoglycemia especially with insulin, kidney problems, gallbladder issues, severe allergic reactions); contraindications (Type 1 diabetes, hypersensitivity to liraglutide, concomitant liraglutide-containing products, <10 years); safety (rotation, no mixing, no sharing). Pricing: 397 SAR. Comparative notes vs Saxenda (liraglutide; different concentration; ≥12 years), Trulicity (dulaglutide; weekly), Ozempic (semaglutide; weekly).[^9]

Allergy: Telfast 180 mg (20 tablets). The page presents fexofenadine hydrochloride 180 mg tablets, with syrup form 30 mg/5 ml; indications (seasonal allergic rhinitis, urticaria; cold symptoms relief); dosing (adults and children ≥12 years: 180 mg once daily for seasonal allergic rhinitis; 60 mg twice daily as needed for allergic rhinitis; urticaria 180 mg once daily); syrup dosing for infants ≥6 months and children 2–12 years; storage (below 25°C in a dry place); contraindications (hypersensitivity; avoid concomitant antacids containing aluminum/magnesium); food interactions (avoid grapefruit, orange, and apple juices); side effects (headache, drowsiness, dizziness, nausea); safety (insufficient pregnancy data—consult physician; lactation—consult physician; non-cortisone). Price: 160 EGP. Comparative positioning vs cetirizine, loratadine, and dimetindene.[^10]

Laxative: Duphalac (lactulose 3.335 g/5 ml; 200 ml). The page outlines a sugar-based osmotic laxative drawing water into intestines to ease stool passage; indications (constipation; treatment/prevention of hepatic encephalopathy; acute constipation); dosing (adults: 1–2 large spoons, preferably after breakfast; single dose; adjust per response; do not exceed recommended dose; do not double on missed dose); onset (~48 hours); common side effects (nausea, mild vomiting resolving in days); serious (severe diarrhea, electrolyte imbalance); pregnancy (consult a doctor); weight gain (not expected). Price: 143 EGP (with an alternative detailed-section price noted as approximately 87 EGP; to be reconciled during QA).[^11]

These exemplars validate the schema and demonstrate field presence across identification, commercial, clinical, and safety domains. They also establish market-specific pricing anchors for EGP and SAR that are essential for downstream normalization.

## Synthesis and Analysis

Clinical completeness. The extracted products demonstrate uneven completeness by class and by page. Floxamo and Sapofen are exemplary in clinical richness—covering indications, dosing, contraindications, side effects, interactions, and precautions—while Augmentin’s page is comparatively sparse, deferring to a leaflet for full prescribing details. This pattern implies that clinical enrichment will rely on both direct page extraction and targeted leaflet retrieval where permissible.

Dosing and safety profiles. The NSAID evidence highlights consistent core safety guidance: use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration, take with food to minimize gastrointestinal irritation, and avoid use in specific populations (e.g., post–heart surgery, peptic ulcer disease). Telfast’s non-sedating profile is emphasized, with explicit interactions to avoid (antacids and certain fruit juices). Victoza requires careful injection-site rotation, prohibits pen sharing, and mandates clinical supervision when used for weight loss in non-diabetics. These class-consistent signals should guide default safety flags for products where fields are missing, pending re-crawl or leaflet confirmation.

Pricing anchors and market differences. The evidence set provides EGP and SAR anchors: Augmentin 117 EGP; Floxamo 110 EGP (pack) and 55 EGP (strip); Victoza 397 SAR; Nurofen children 17.15 SAR; Sapofen 400 10 SAR; Telfast 180 160 EGP; Duphalac 143 EGP. Market differences (e.g., Saudi syrup pricing vs Egyptian tablet pricing) necessitate currency tagging and market-aware comparators. In QA, pricing anomalies (e.g., Duphalac alternative price in the detailed section) must be reconciled with source preference rules.

Category mapping. Controlled vocabulary mapping is robust for antibiotics (penicillin combinations), NSAIDs, GLP-1 agonists, and osmotic laxatives. Subcategory hints are useful where brand pages are sparse (e.g., allergy class corroborated by Telfast). The mapping pipeline should default to class-level labels (e.g., “Antibiotic (Penicillin combination)”) to support analytics and clinical grouping.

