### Operational Efficiency Measures

- Batch searches by therapeutic class, concurrent extractions, and tight retry policies to maximize throughput.
- Rapid discrepancy resolution (e.g., missing clinical fields) via targeted re-crawls and subcategory cross-checks.
- Logging of failed URLs with classification (client error, server error, content sparse) and scheduled retries.

These measures, combined with strict provenance tracking, ensure progress even under constrained execution steps.

## Data Quality, Validation, and Acceptance Criteria

A multi-stage QA pipeline is applied:

- Deduplication. Composite key: normalized product_name + strength + pack_size + brand_name. Merge diacritics/spacing variants; retain aliases.
- Category normalization. Controlled vocabulary mapped; subcategory used as tie-breaker.
- Prescription flag. Defaults by class: antibiotics, antihypertensives (especially combinations), anticoagulants, GLP-1 agonists → likely prescription; antihistamines, antacids, simple laxatives → likely OTC; flag exceptions for clinical review.
- Acceptance tests. Schema compliance, anchor verification (product 1401), URL validity (sample), price format normalization, controlled vocabulary mapping, and duplicate scan.

Table 6 codifies these rules for production.

Table 6. Validation Rules Matrix

| Rule | Type | Action | Example |
|---|---|---|---|
| Name canonicalization | Normalization | Strip diacritics, collapse whitespace | “اوجمنتين” → “اوجمنتين” |
| Strength normalization | Normalization | Enforce “X mg,” “X mcg,” “X%” | “625جم” → “625 mg” |
| Pack size parsing | Normalization | “N units” | “10 قرص” → “10 tablets” |
| Duplicate detection | QC | Merge duplicates; retain aliases | Same brand + strength + pack size |
| Category mapping | QC | Map to controlled vocabulary | “Antibiotic (Penicillin combination)” |
| Prescription inference | QC | Set boolean; flag exceptions | Victoza → true |
| Price parsing | QC | Numeric; currency tag | “117” EGP → 117 EGP |

